<TopicPage slug="subjects/geography/advanced/economic-globalisation">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    The geography sequence so far has read the physical stage — Latitude and Climate, Plate
    Tectonics, Ecosystems and Biomes — and then the human patterns laid across it, above all
    in Urban Geography &amp; Cities and Development and Inequality, which introduced the
    gaps this lesson's flows widen and close. Economic globalisation is the connecting
    story: how production, money and visitors came to move across borders, who captures the
    gains, who bears the pain, and how the map is now being redrawn.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>read employment structures through the Clark–Fisher model, including premature deindustrialisation and the informal economy;</li>
    <li>explain containers, global value chains and special economic zones as the machinery of production;</li>
    <li>apply comparative advantage and value-chain logic to tariffs and trade conflicts;</li>
    <li>analyse tourism through the Butler life-cycle, leakage and overtourism;</li>
    <li>interpret trade openness, FDI and migration as integration measures — with their cautions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Economic globalisation is <strong>the integration of production, trade, capital and labour
    across borders</strong> — and its modern machinery is more concrete than the word suggests:
    a steel box (the container), fibre-optic cables, container ships carrying over 20,000
    standard units, and trade rules agreed at the World Trade Organization. Measured simply,
    world trade in goods and services has risen from about a quarter of global output in 1970
    to around 60% today. It is the most powerful poverty-reduction machine in history — close
    to a billion people left extreme poverty between 1990 and the late 2010s, most of them in
    Asia, on globalised trade — and simultaneously the most efficient disruption machine,
    hollowing out industrial towns an ocean away, usually in the same decade. Its current
    chapter is not retreat but <strong>reorganisation</strong>: chips, batteries and
    pharmaceuticals have been reclassified as strategic, and efficiency is ceding ground to
    resilience. This chapter works through the three instruments the syllabus names:
    employment structures, the globalisation-and-TNC engine, and tourism.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="call the trade share before the reveal" widget="estimate" config={egTradeEstimate} />

  <Widget no="1" caption="the machinery, the map, and the rewiring" widget="equationStep" config={globCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four steps tell the story in sequence — a box cut
    trade costs, production dispersed into chains, gains and pains landed unevenly, and
    security policy is now redrawing the map. Hold step 2 against the phone example later
    in the lesson: once components cross borders repeatedly, a tariff taxes your own
    factories too, and gross trade statistics begin to mislead.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> globalisation is not a mood but machinery — containers, cables
    and rules that made distance cheap. The first place that machinery shows up is in what
    people do for a living.
  </p>

  <h2>Changing employment structures</h2>
  <p>
    Development has a signature in <em>what people do for a living</em>. The Clark–Fisher
    sector model tracks employment through three sectors: <strong>primary</strong> (farming,
    fishing, mining), <strong>secondary</strong> (manufacturing) and <strong>tertiary</strong>
    (services), and the universal development path runs primary → secondary → tertiary. The
    United Kingdom walked it first and furthest: from the world's cotton and steel workshop in
    1850, through manufacturing employment's long fall since the 1960s, to an economy where
    over 80% of jobs are now in services — deindustrialisation as the visible face of moving
    up the ladder, with painful regional scars (the coalfields, the mill towns). Newly
    industrialising countries show the middle of the sequence: Bangladesh moved its economy
    decisively into the secondary sector, with roughly four million garment workers — the
    majority women — producing about 80% of export earnings. But the newest pattern is the
    most interesting: some late developers leapfrog manufacturing into services directly.
    India's economy now earns over half its output from services while manufacturing stays
    near 15% — Rodrik's <strong>"premature deindustrialisation"</strong>, possible because
    software and business services can be exported without a factory stage. That matters
    because manufacturing was historically the escalator that absorbed millions of low-skill
    workers at scale; services are larger but more unequal in entry requirements. Below the
    formal sectors everywhere sits the <strong>informal economy</strong> — unregistered work
    from street vending to gig platforms — which official employment statistics undercount by
    half or more in much of the developing world, a reminder that "employment structure"
    figures are the visible top of a deeper labour iceberg.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="file each economy under its sector signature" widget="sort-buckets" config={egSectorSort} />

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> employment tells the development story — fields to factories to
    services, with some late developers skipping the factory stage entirely. The next
    section explains the engine that moved the factories in the first place.
  </p>

  <h2>Globalisation and transnational corporations</h2>
  <p>
    The engine has three parts. <strong>Trade-cost collapse.</strong> Before 1956, loading a
    ship was slow, skilled, pilferage-prone work costing several dollars a ton. Malcolm
    McLean's standardized container turned loading into craned steel boxes and cut port
    handling costs by more than 90% — Levinson's accounting puts it from $5.86 to about 16
    cents per ton. When ships grew to 24,000 TEU and the Ever Given's grounding in the Suez
    Canal (2021) could hold up trade of the order of $10 billion a day, that is the scale the
    box built. <strong>Global value chains.</strong> Once moving goods became cheap,
    <em>production itself</em> dispersed: about half of world trade now flows inside value
    chains where components cross borders repeatedly before the final product ships. The
    phone in your pocket is the textbook artefact — designed in California, silicon from
    Taiwan and South Korea, assembled by Foxconn at Zhengzhou in a complex employing on the
    order of 200,000 people — and the value accounting is the revision every student needs: a
    classic study found China captured only a few dollars of value from each early iPhone
    assembled there, because most of the price was American design, Japanese and Korean
    components, and Taiwanese chips. "Imports from China" are partly imports from everywhere,
    which is why economists now measure trade in value added rather than gross flows.
    <strong>Policy instruments.</strong> Transnational corporations — firms coordinating
    production in several countries, from Nestlé to Toyota to Apple — locate where costs,
    skills, tariffs and markets direct them, and states compete for that location with
    <strong>special economic zones</strong>: over 5,400 worldwide, with Shenzhen (1980, a
    fishing town turned 17-million metropolis) as the founding miracle and Intel's 1997 Costa
    Rica plant — which at its 1999 peak produced over a third of the country's exports — as
    the small-country version. The same tools can wound: the "China shock" literature
    estimates that surging Chinese import competition cost the US on the order of two million
    jobs between 1999 and 2011, concentrated in specific manufacturing regions — aggregate
    gains, geographically concentrated pain, which is precisely the political fault line of
    our decade.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="call the cost collapse per ton" widget="estimate" config={egBoxEstimate} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "comparative advantage means each country should do what it
    is best at." Ricardo's actual insight is subtler and stronger: gains from trade depend on
    <em>opportunity cost</em>, not absolute skill. Suppose one worker-day yields either 4 phones
    or 2 tonnes of wheat in country A, and either 1 phone or 1 tonne of wheat in country B. A
    is better at making both — yet B should still specialise in wheat, because B gives up only
    1 phone per tonne of wheat while A gives up 2. Trade at any rate between those bounds
    benefits both countries. This is why low-productivity economies integrate into world trade
    successfully, and why "we can't compete with them at anything" is an argument against the
    theory, not with it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "tariffs hurt the foreign exporter and protect us." In a
    value-chain world, tariffs land on <em>inputs</em>: a 25% tariff on a $400 imported
    component raises your own factories' costs by $100 a unit before any foreign firm feels
    anything, and consumers share the bill through prices. Both sides of a trade war pay;
    the argument is about who pays less.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where production lands is also a governance question, and two cases frame it. Value
    chains reach into agriculture as much as electronics: a large share of Europe's
    supermarket roses are grown around Kenya's Lake Naivasha and flown out fresh — high-value
    perishables made possible by the same combination of cheap air freight and cold chains
    that moved the phone — bringing employment and irrigation pressure in equal measure. And
    when the chains are long, accountability gets short: the Rana Plaza garment-factory
    collapse in Bangladesh (2013), which killed 1,134 workers producing for Western brands,
    exposed the gap between a brand's code of conduct and the subcontracted building it
    never inspected, and produced the Bangladesh Accord on factory safety as the template
    for binding supply-chain regulation. The lesson generalises: a TNC's map is a network of
    contracts, and the geography of responsibility does not automatically follow the geography
    of production — somebody has to build the institutions that make it.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="pair the governance cases with their lessons" widget="match" config={egCasesMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the production engine is boxes, chains and competing policy —
    generating aggregate gains with geographically concentrated pain. The next flow moves
    people rather than parts: tourism, globalisation's most visible traffic.
  </p>

  <h2>Tourism: a growth industry</h2>
  <p>
    Tourism is globalisation's most visible flow — people, not parts. The scale curve is
    steep: about 25 million international arrivals in 1950, roughly 1.5 billion in 2019,
    supporting on the order of one in ten jobs worldwide. COVID-19 then performed the largest
    natural experiment in the industry's history — arrivals collapsed by about 70% in 2020,
    emptying Venice and grounding the Maldives' economy — before recovery to about 1.4 billion
    arrivals in 2024, essentially the 2019 peak. The industry's economics have a geography
    every student should be able to read. <strong>Growth follows the Butler life-cycle:</strong>
    destinations pass from exploration through involvement and development to consolidation,
    stagnation and then rejuvenation or decline — Benidorm moving from fishing village to
    package-tourism archetype to high-rise rejuvenation is the standard worked case.
    <strong>Benefits leak.</strong> Gross tourist spending is not local income: food
    imported to feed hotels, profits repatriated by foreign-owned chains, and booking-platform
    commissions all drain away, and in some small island states studies have estimated
    leakage as high as half of receipts. <strong>Costs concentrate.</strong> Overtourism has
    made residents' movements news — Barcelona's protests, and Venice's €5 day-tripper entry
    fee from 2024, are management responses to a classic congestion problem — while tourism's
    carbon footprint is estimated at about 8% of global greenhouse-gas emissions, mostly from
    flights. <strong>Dependence cuts both ways:</strong> the Maldives earns around a quarter
    of its GDP directly from tourism, which made the 2020 border closures an economic
    emergency, and makes "diversify the economy" the standing advice every tourism-dependent
    state hears and struggles to follow.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="rebuild the Butler life-cycle" widget="order" config={egButlerOrder} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "more tourists automatically means more money for local
    people." Measure receipts net of leakage, seasonality and the low-wage structure of much
    tourism employment, and the correlation weakens sharply. The right questions are always:
    who owns the hotel, where is the food bought, what share of the booking fee stayed in
    the country.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="true or false — tourists, money, locals" widget="judge" config={egLeakageJudge} />

  <h2>Measuring integration: the indicators</h2>
  <p>
    Claims this large need numbers, and the discipline has four workhorses. The
    <strong>trade openness ratio</strong> — exports plus imports as a share of GDP — is the
    headline measure, and comparing it across countries is an instant lesson in
    interpretation: the Netherlands runs a ratio above 150%, the United States around 25%,
    and the difference is not that the Dutch trade "six times more enthusiastically" — it is
    that a small economy near the sea with big ports lives by re-exporting, while a
    continental economy produces for its own vast market. Ratios measure exposure, not
    vitality. <strong>Foreign direct investment</strong> (FDI) tracks where firms are
    <em>buying into</em> production — building or acquiring factories, mines and offices — and
    its flows map the geography of confidence better than trade does. <strong>Migration
    stocks</strong> (people living outside their country of birth) measure labour
    integration; the financial layer is read through <strong>capital flows and reserve
    movements</strong>. Two cautions belong to every one of them: integration measures move
    with the price of oil and the exchange rate as much as with policy, and — as the
    value-added correction showed — the standard gross measures systematically flatter
    countries that sit at the end of long chains. A serious answer to "how globalised is
    this economy?" always asks: measured how, gross or value-added, relative to what.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="drag the divider — two openness ratios" widget="compare" config={egOpennessCompare} />

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the indicators measure exposure — and each needs its cautions,
    from gross-versus-value-added accounting to price effects. The last flow moves fastest
    of all: money itself.
  </p>

  <h2>The financial layer: faster and more fragile</h2>
  <p>
    Money globalised faster than goods, and it shows. Capital now moves in seconds, and the
    three crises that mark the era — Asia 1997, when hot money reversed out of "tiger"
    economies within months; 2008, when US mortgage defaults became a global banking freeze;
    2022, when rate rises repriced every emerging-market debt overnight — all share one
    geography: financial contagion travels by balance sheet, not by road, so it crosses borders
    faster than any container. Trade globalisation is stable and slow; financial globalisation
    is volatile and fast — which is why the IMF exists as a crisis lender, and why developing
    economies that open their capital accounts before their banking systems can bear it have
    repeatedly paid for the sequencing error.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="run the central bank — sequence the opening" widget="branch" config={egCapitalBranch} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>State Ricardo's comparative advantage in terms of opportunity cost, and explain why a country that is worse at producing everything can still gain from trade.</li>
    <li>A country imposes a 25% tariff on imported electronic components that its own factories assemble into finished goods. Who feels the first-round cost, and why do modern tariff wars injure their own industries?</li>
    <li>An island state reports record tourist arrivals. Give two reasons why local income may not rise in proportion, and name the concept that links them.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) What matters is not absolute skill but what each country
    gives up: specialise where your opportunity cost is lowest. In the worked example,
    country B sacrifices only 1 phone per tonne of wheat against country A's 2, so B gains
    by growing wheat and trading — at any exchange rate between the two opportunity costs,
    both countries end up better off. (2) The country's own factories feel it first:
    components cross borders repeatedly inside value chains — roughly half of world trade —
    so the tariff raises domestic producers' input costs before any foreign exporter adjusts.
    Both sides of a trade war pay; the argument is about who pays less. (3) Leakage first:
    imported food, foreign-owned hotels and booking commissions drain receipts — estimated
    as high as half in some small island states. Second, dependence and seasonality: gross
    spending is not stable local income, and overtourism brings congestion costs that
    management (Venice's entry fee, Barcelona's protests) must pay for.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Globalisation is machinery — containers, value chains and trade rules that made distance cheap.</li>
    <li>Employment structures tell the development story; premature deindustrialisation skips the factory escalator.</li>
    <li>Comparative advantage is about opportunity cost, not absolute skill — the common error is using the wrong test.</li>
    <li>In value-chain economies, tariffs tax your own imports; both sides of a trade war pay.</li>
    <li>TNCs locate where costs, skills and policy direct; special economic zones moved the map of production within a generation.</li>
    <li>Tourism receipts must be read net of leakage, seasonality and congestion costs.</li>
    <li>Integration measures need cautions: gross flows flatter chain-end economies, and financial contagion travels fastest of all.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Population &amp; Migration Geography follows the labour flow that this lesson's measures
    only count, Resource Provision: Food and Energy traces the commodity flows that value
    chains move, and Remote Sensing &amp; Earth Observation supplies the measurement tools
    behind the maps of a rewiring world.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Globalisation is not new.</strong> The first globalisation (roughly 1870–1914) ran
    on steamships, railways, the telegraph and the gold standard; by 1914 trade's share of
    world output reached levels not seen again until the late twentieth century, and tens of
    millions of Europeans emigrated under near-open borders. The 1914–45 era of wars and
    tariffs destroyed it — the standing proof that integration is a policy choice that
    politics can reverse. <strong>Uneven development, updated.</strong> Dependency theory's
    core–periphery map has been redrawn — China is now a core economy and the largest trading
    partner of over 120 countries — but the underlying asymmetry persists: commodity exporters
    still ride price cycles they do not set, and the Prebisch–Singer argument that primary
    export prices trend down against manufactures remains the standing critique of
    commodity-led development. <strong>The measurement frontier.</strong> Gross trade flows
    systematically mislead in value-chain economies (the iPhone problem); trade-in-value-added
    datasets (TiVA) reassign value to where it was actually created, and they routinely
    rewrite bilateral deficits by a third or more. <strong>The rewiring.</strong> Since 2022,
    export controls on advanced semiconductors, the US CHIPS Act ($52.7 billion of subsidies)
    and the EU Chips Act (€43 billion) amount to industrial policy by another name, and
    "friend-shoring" is redirecting flows through <em>connector economies</em> — Vietnam and
    Mexico's trade surges are partly Chinese and American production re-routed. Goods trade as
    a share of world GDP peaked around the 2008 crisis and has plateaued since ("slowbalisation");
    services and data flows kept growing. <strong>Open questions:</strong> whether resilience
    is worth its price (duplicate supply chains are insurance, and insurance has a premium);
    whether connector economies climb the value ladder or get squeezed between the blocs; and
    how to govern digital trade — the largest flow no customs officer ever weighs.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself" widget="quiz" config={globQuizCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four questions test the lesson's core machinery.
    The first is the comparative-advantage discipline — opportunity cost, never absolute
    skill. The second is the value-chain correction applied to tariffs. The third and
    fourth are geography's own cases: tourism's leakage and the Shenzhen demonstration that
    policy, not just location, moves the map.
  </p>

